Assessment Test: Software Tester (QA)

1. Registration Testing

Positive Test Cases
TC-01: Register with valid details
Test: Enter a valid name, email address, password, and confirm password, then click Register.
Expected Result: The account should be created successfully and the user should see a success
message or be taken to the login/dashboard page.

TC-02: Register with a valid email and strong password
Test: Enter a properly formatted email and a password that meets the application's password
requirements.
Expected Result: The registration should be completed successfully.
Negative Test Cases

TC-03: Register without entering any details
Test: Leave all registration fields empty and click Register.
Expected Result: The application should show clear validation messages for the required fields and
should not create an account.

TC-04: Register with an invalid email address
Test: Enter an invalid email such as test@ or test.com.
Expected Result: The application should show an email validation message and should not complete
registration.

TC-05: Register with an already registered email
Test: Use an email address that is already present in the system.
Expected Result: A second account should not be created. The user should receive a clear message
that the email is already registered.

TC-06: Register with a weak password
Test: Enter a very short or weak password such as 12345.
Expected Result: The application should reject it and explain the password requirements.

TC-07: Password and confirm password do not match
Test: Enter different values in the password and confirm password fields.
Expected Result: Registration should fail and the user should be told that both passwords must
match.
Edge Cases

TC-08: Enter only spaces in required fields
Test: Enter spaces instead of a real name or email address.
Expected Result: The application should treat the input as invalid rather than accepting spaces as
valid data.

TC-09: Enter a very long name or email
Test: Enter input that is much longer than a normal name or email.
Expected Result: The application should handle it safely without crashing, breaking the page, or
storing corrupted data.

TC-10: Enter special characters
Test: Enter special characters in registration fields where appropriate.
Expected Result: The application should validate the input correctly and handle it safely.

2. Login Testing
Positive Test Cases
TC-11: Login with valid credentials
Test: Enter a registered email address and the correct password.
Expected Result: The user should be logged in successfully and taken to the task management page
or dashboard.

TC-12: Login immediately after registration
Test: Register a new user and then use the same credentials to log in.
Expected Result: The newly created user should be able to log in successfully.
Negative Test Cases

TC-13: Login with an incorrect password
Test: Enter a valid email address but use the wrong password.
Expected Result: Login should fail and a suitable error message should be displayed.

TC-14: Login with an unregistered email
Test: Enter an email address that does not exist in the application.
Expected Result: The user should not be logged in and should receive an appropriate error message.

TC-15: Login with empty fields
Test: Leave both email and password empty and click Login.
Expected Result: Required-field validation messages should be displayed.


TC-16: Login with an invalid email format
Test: Enter an email such as abc@.
Expected Result: The application should display an email validation error.
Edge Cases

TC-17: Check password case sensitivity
Test: Enter the correct password with different uppercase and lowercase characters.
Expected Result: Login should fail when the password does not exactly match, if the application
treats passwords as case-sensitive.

TC-18: Multiple incorrect login attempts
Test: Try to log in repeatedly using an incorrect password.
Expected Result: The application should handle repeated failures securely and should not reveal
sensitive information.

TC-19: Refresh after successful login
Test: Log in successfully and refresh the page.
Expected Result: The user's session should continue according to the application's expected session
behavior.

TC-20: Access the task page after logout
Test: Log out and then try to open the protected task page directly.
Expected Result: The user should not be able to access protected task functionality after logout.

3. Task Creation Testing

TC-21: Create a task with valid information
Test: Enter a valid task title and any other required information, then click Create.
Expected Result: The task should be created successfully and should appear in the task list.

TC-22: Create a task without a required title
Test: Leave the required task title empty and try to create the task.
Expected Result: A clear validation message should appear and the task should not be created.

TC-23: Create a task with optional fields empty
Test: Leave optional fields blank while providing the required information.
Expected Result: If those fields are optional, the task should still be created successfully.


TC-24: Create multiple tasks

Test: Create several different tasks one after another.
Expected Result: All tasks should be saved and displayed correctly without replacing or losing earlier
tasks.

TC-25: Create a task with a very long title
Test: Enter a title at or beyond the expected maximum length.
Expected Result: The application should handle the limit properly without crashing or damaging the
layout.

TC-26: Create a task with special characters
Test: Enter a task containing characters such as &, <, >, quotes, or other special characters.
Expected Result: The task should be stored and displayed correctly, and the input should be handled
safely.

4. View Task List Testing
TC-27: View the task list after login
Test: Log in and open the task list.
Expected Result: The logged-in user's tasks should be displayed correctly.

TC-28: View the task list when there are no tasks
Test: Use a new account with no tasks and open the task list.
Expected Result: A helpful empty-state message such as 'No tasks found' should be shown instead of
a broken or confusing screen.

TC-29: Create a task and refresh
Test: Create a task and then refresh the page.
Expected Result: The task should still be visible because it should have been saved in the database.

TC-30: Verify that users only see their own tasks
Test: Create tasks with two different user accounts and compare the task lists.
Expected Result: Each user should only see their own tasks. One user must not be able to view
another user's tasks.

TC-31: View a large number of tasks
Test: Create or load a large number of tasks.
Expected Result: The list should load correctly without missing, duplicating, or incorrectly displaying
records.

5. Edit Task Testing


TC-32: Edit an existing task
Test: Open an existing task, change its information, and save it.
Expected Result: The task should be updated successfully and the new information should be
displayed.

TC-33: Remove required information while editing
Test: Edit a task and remove a required value before saving.
Expected Result: The application should show a validation message and should not save invalid data.

TC-34: Cancel an edit
Test: Open a task for editing, make a change, and then cancel.
Expected Result: The original task information should remain unchanged.
TC-35: Edit with maximum-length input
Test: Enter data at the maximum allowed length.
Expected Result: The application should handle the boundary correctly without crashing or
truncating data unexpectedly.
TC-36: Refresh after editing
Test: Edit and save a task, then refresh the page.
Expected Result: The updated information should still be present after the refresh.
6. Delete Task Testing
TC-37: Delete an existing task
Test: Select an existing task and choose Delete.
Expected Result: The selected task should be removed from the task list and from the database.
TC-38: Cancel the delete operation
Test: If a confirmation message is shown, select Cancel.
Expected Result: The task should remain unchanged and should not be deleted.
TC-39: Delete multiple tasks
Test: Delete more than one task where the application allows it.
Expected Result: Only the selected tasks should be deleted; all other tasks should remain
unchanged.
TC-40: Refresh after deletion
Test: Delete a task and then refresh the page.
Expected Result: The deleted task should not appear again.
TC-41: Try to delete another user's task
Test: Attempt to delete a task that belongs to a different user.
Expected Result: The application should block the operation because a user should only manage
their own tasks.


7. Input Validation and Error Handling
I would also check how the application behaves when the user provides unexpected input or when a
system dependency such as the server, database, or network is unavailable.
TC-42: Submit forms with empty required fields
Test: Submit registration, login, or task forms without completing required fields.
Expected Result: The application should display clear and useful validation messages.

TC-43: Enter an invalid email format
Test: Use an invalid email value in registration or login.
Expected Result: The email should be rejected with a clear validation message.
TC-44: Enter extremely long input
Test: Paste a very large amount of text into a field.
Expected Result: The application should enforce reasonable limits or safely handle the input without
crashing.
TC-45: Enter HTML or JavaScript in a task field
Test: Enter HTML or script content in a task title or description.
Expected Result: The application should treat it as data and should not execute the script.
TC-46: Enter malicious database-related input
Test: Provide database-related malicious input through fields such as email, password, or task data.
Expected Result: The input should be handled safely. It should not expose database details or allow
unauthorized database operations.
TC-47: Database or server becomes unavailable
Test: Try an operation while the database or server is unavailable.
Expected Result: The application should show a friendly error message instead of crashing or
exposing technical details.
TC-48: Network failure during task creation
Test: Interrupt the network while creating a task and then retry.
Expected Result: The user should be informed that the operation failed, and the same task should
not accidentally be created more than once.
TC-49: Session expires during a task operation
Test: Allow the login session to expire and then try to create, edit, or delete a task.
Expected Result: The application should ask the user to log in again and should not allow an
unauthorized operation.
TC-50: Click Create/Submit multiple times
Test: Click the Create or Submit button several times quickly.
Expected Result: The application should prevent accidental duplicate submissions and duplicate task
records.


8. Potential Bugs / Risk Areas
The following are risks I would specifically look for during testing. These are potential issues identified
from the application's described functionality, not bugs that I have confirmed by executing the
application.

1. User can see another user's tasks
Severity: Critical
Description: There may be a problem with authorization or user filtering that allows one user to see
another user's tasks.
Impact: This is a serious privacy and security issue because personal task information could be
exposed.
2. Unauthorized users can access task functionality
Severity: Critical
Description: A person who is not logged in may be able to directly access task pages or APIs.
Impact: An unauthorized person could potentially create, edit, or delete data without permission.
3. Passwords are not handled securely
Severity: Critical
Description: There may be a risk that passwords are stored or transmitted in an insecure way.
Impact: If passwords are exposed, attackers could gain access to user accounts.
4. SQL Injection vulnerability
Severity: Critical
Description: User input may not be safely handled before being used in database queries.
Impact: An attacker could manipulate database queries, access unauthorized information, or
damage data.
5. Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)
Severity: Major
Description: Task fields may accept HTML or JavaScript and display it without proper escaping.
Impact: A malicious script could execute when someone views the task, creating a security risk.
6. Deleted task appears again after refresh
Severity: Major
Description: A task may disappear from the screen after deletion but come back after refreshing.
Impact: This could mean the deletion was not correctly saved in the database and would lead to data
inconsistency.
7. Edited task changes are not saved
Severity: Major
Description: The updated task may look correct temporarily but show the old data after a refresh.
Impact: Users may believe their changes were saved when they were actually lost.

8. Duplicate tasks are created
Severity: Major
Description: Clicking Create multiple times quickly may result in the same task being stored more
than once.
Impact: This can create duplicate records and make the task list inaccurate.
9. Application crashes when the database is unavailable
Severity: Major
Description: The application may crash or show a technical error when it cannot connect to the
database.
Impact: Users may be unable to use the application and may not understand how to recover.
10. Invalid or empty data is accepted
Severity: Minor to Major
Description: The application may accept missing or invalid values for fields that should be required.
Impact: This can create incomplete or incorrect records and may cause problems in other parts of
the system.
9. Overall Testing Approach
Because the application is close to production, I would not only check whether each feature works in
the normal flow. I would also focus on security, data accuracy, usability, and how the system behaves
when something goes wrong.
1. First, verify the complete registration and login flow.
2. Check authentication and authorization to make sure only permitted users can access task data.
3. Test all four main task operations: create, view, edit, and delete.
4. Test valid, invalid, empty, boundary, and unexpected inputs.
5. Verify that task information is correctly saved and retrieved from the database.
6. Make sure one user cannot access or modify another user's tasks.
7. Test error handling for network, server, and database failures.
8. Perform basic security testing for issues such as XSS and SQL injection.
9. Check the application on common browsers and different screen sizes.
10. Perform regression testing after defects are fixed to make sure existing features still work.
10. Conclusion
Before recommending the application for production release, I would make sure that the core
functionality works reliably and that there are no critical security, authorization, or data-loss issues. I
would give the highest priority to authentication, authorization, user data isolation, password
security, and database operations.
In my view, good QA is not only about checking whether the application works when everything goes
as expected. It is also about checking how the application behaves when users enter incorrect
information, perform unexpected actions, submit requests repeatedly, or when a system dependency
fails. The goal is to make sure the application is reliable, secure, and easy for users to work with
